# Claim 07 — Checkpoint / recoverability

## Goal
Verify whether the system creates local checkpoint commits or other
recoverability points before risky steps. Use the wording "local checkpoint
commits" or "recoverability checkpoints" — never "pushes to git".

## Static inspection

### What is a "checkpoint" here

A checkpoint is a per-task pair (recorded into the run's session state)
that captures:
1. an autosave commit of any dirty working-tree state immediately before
   the task starts (the "beforeCommit"), and
2. the resulting HEAD SHA (the "baseSha") — the precise point the worker
   will be reverted to if recovery is needed.

This is a local-only mechanism: it lives inside the working-tree git
repository on the host that ran the goal. It never invokes `git push`
on its own during the per-task loop; the only push path
(`pushRunBranch`) is gated by an explicit `isRepoPrivate` check and is
called separately from `gh pr create` plumbing — not from per-task
recovery.

