### What it is NOT

- It is NOT a git push of any kind during the task loop. `pushRunBranch`
  (src/goal/git-checkpoint.ts:211-238) exists, but is privacy-gated
  (`isRepoPrivate` at :221) and is invoked only when a separate
  PR-creation flow runs, not during per-task recovery.
- It is NOT a stash. No `git stash` calls anywhere in
  git-checkpoint.ts.
- It is NOT a database snapshot. Disk-only, plain git.

## Safe README wording
- "Before each task starts, the system creates a **local checkpoint commit**
  on a per-run branch so worker changes can be reverted if recovery is
  needed."
- "On failure, the executor restores the working tree to the task's
  recorded **recoverability checkpoint** via `git reset --hard <baseSha>`
  and lets the worker retry from a known-good state."
- "Checkpoint commits and the per-run branch stay local; nothing is
  pushed to a remote unless you explicitly opt in."

## Unsafe README wording to avoid
- "automatically pushes to git after each step"  ← FALSE.
- "auto-pushes to your origin"                    ← FALSE.
- "git stash"                                     ← not the mechanism in use.
- "snapshots your entire repo"                    ← it commits dirty state, doesn't snapshot ignored files / untracked-and-ignored.

STATUS: verified
REASON: Per-task local checkpoint commits are implemented and wired into the executor's task loop. `startTaskCheckpoint` (git-checkpoint.ts:296) runs an `autosaveIfDirty` plus `getHeadSha` at task entry; `finalizeTaskCheckpoint` (git-checkpoint.ts:311) commits successful worker output; `resetToTaskBaseSha` (build-gate.ts:195) reverts on failure. The executor wires all three at agent-executor.ts:388, 465, 499, and 609. No `git push` is invoked from the per-task recovery path. Safe README wording is "local checkpoint commits" or "recoverability checkpoints".
